<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Central and Southern Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Sultanate of Bangassou</h1><p class="st-meta">1860 CE – 1917 CE (approx.) · capital Bangassou</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Nzakara sultanate on the Mbomou River, a slave-raiding state that survived by dealing with both Belgian and French colonisers.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nzakara">Nzakara</a> state on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mbomou-river">Mbomou River</a> was ruled by a Bandia dynasty related to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zande-people">Zande</a> kingdoms. In the later 19th century its ruler Bangassou, whose name was given to the state and its capital, built it into a regional power.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Bangassou took captives and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> from neighbouring peoples and sold them to northern traders from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> basin and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-dar-al-kuti">Dar al Kuti</a>, acquiring guns in return. Visitors described a large court with many wives and dependants. Power rested on control of trade and on armed retainers rather than on fixed borders.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Bangassou signed a treaty with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/congo-free-state">Congo Free State</a> in 1890, but the 1894 Franco-Congolese agreement put his capital on the French side. He died in 1907, and the French reduced the sultanate to an administrative chiefdom within <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ubangi-shari">Ubangi-Shari</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="sultanate, 1860 CE – 1917 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V62 Q24 40 50 34 Q76 40 76 62 V82 Z"/><path d="M50 34 V24"/><path d="M53 13 A6 6 0 1 0 53 25 A4.5 4.5 0 1 1 53 13"/><path d="M38 82 V66 Q44 58 50 66 V82"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">sultanate</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Central and Southern Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:96.72%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1860 CE – 1917 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bangassou</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bangassou</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Nzakara</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/central-african-republic">Central African Republic</a>, <a href="/countries/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Bangassou, Nzakara sultanate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1888&amp;focus=sultanate-of-bangassou" aria-label="Sultanate of Bangassou on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5313 2000 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sultanate of Bangassou lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5633 2256L5515 2270"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5515 2270h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5633 2256h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5633 2256h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bangassou. <a href="/atlas?year=1888&amp;focus=sultanate-of-bangassou">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

